Item 07 is done: the customer search is now modelled in Lean, with no `sorry`, and the gate passes (1293 tests passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed; `sorry` count still 1, the §24 conjecture). It is marked `[x]`, and PROGRESS.md now reads 7/13 with the Iteration 7 section added. Nothing was committed.

**What the Lean proves** (`lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/Basic.lean`, imported from the root; axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` only):
- **Definitions:** closed and opened sets, the cost of one closing step and of a whole closing order, `Solvable G k T` (some ordering of the customers not yet closed costs at most k), and the search's own predicate `SearchSol`. `SearchSol` is defined the way the code recurses, not in terms of `Solvable`, so linking the two is a real theorem.
- **Monotonicity:** closing more customers without opening more stacks never hurts (`solvable_mono`).
- **The free move is sound:** closing a customer whose neighbours are all already open never hurts (`solvable_insert_of_free`). The reverse direction also holds when at most k stacks are open at the node.
- **Lemma F:** under that same condition, `Solvable` from T is equivalent to `SearchSol` from its free closure (`solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`). One direction needs no condition.
- **Cost = narrowness:** a full closing order costs exactly the narrowness of the matching sequence in `Narrowness.lean`, which equals its vertex separation plus 1.
- **The search decides MOSP:** `SearchSol` from the empty set holds exactly when pathwidth + 1 ≤ k, and on the MOSP graph exactly when `mospValue ≤ k` (`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`). So a refutation can only mean `mospValue > k`.

**Checked before stating:** `python -m paper2.search_check --model` copies the Lean definitions directly into Python. It covers every labelled graph on 0–6 vertices and every graph on 7 vertices (34,912 graphs), with every layout and every k. It found zero failures across 29.0M layouts, 91.5M monotonicity pairs, 33.0M free moves and 18.2M Lemma F cases. It takes 48 s on 30 cores and writes `paper2/data/search_model_check.json`.

The "at most k open" condition is necessary: without it, Lemma F fails in 3.55M cases. The smallest is a single edge with one customer closed at k = 0. Four new tests in `tests/test_search_check.py` cover this case, hand-computed values and a deliberately broken cost function.

**Docs:** new §4.1 in `paper2/search_soundness.md` has the definitions table, each theorem with its proof idea, modelling notes and the check table.

